{"kind":"report","text":"APPROVED at exact head 076727fed7677931f173654a61a127975380e487, covering all four live candidate artifacts (internal/workroom fa20f4c5, docs eb34cd7e, AGENTS.md 472da802, SKILL.md c6564f79). This is a delta review against my round-1 reviewed head 546b3e39, whose changes-requested report was 903c37b9.\n\nBOTH RATIFIED FINDINGS ARE GENUINELY REPAIRED.\n\nFinding 1, docs/README.md. The work-loop row now reads \"How a promise becomes an exact artifact report, how an independently approved merge closes it, and when explicit reports still apply.\" That covers all three things the old line omitted or misstated: artifact-as-report, independently approved merge closure, and the surviving explicit-report path. It is coherent with the page's own front-matter summary.\n\nFinding 2, the merger-identity guard. I repeated my round-1 mutation in an isolated worktree at this exact head: deleting \"artifact.record.Actor != receipt.record.Actor\" from mergedArtifacts now FAILS TestMergeDoesNotCloseAnotherAuthorsCommitment. In round 1 that same deletion survived the entire suite. I also re-ran my other two mutations to confirm no regression in the existing pins: dropping the promisor-authorship condition still fails TestOnlyThePromisorsSinglePromiseArtifactActsAsAReport, and dropping !f.ratified(approvalID) still fails TestMergeDoesNotCloseCommitmentWithoutExplicitApprovalRatification. All three seams are now pinned; the mutation worktree was restored and verified clean.\n\nThe new test is well constructed and models exactly the widening I raised: a bystander implements internal/workroom/fold.go at the same head under their own request and promise, the reviewer cites both artifacts, and the primary implementer signs the merge receipt with both artifacts in the retirement plan (they share a tree lineage, so sameTreeLineage admits the foreign path). It asserts the merger's own commitment reaches satisfied while the bystander's stays reported and waiting on its requester. That is the precise authority boundary.\n\nANYTHING ELSE CHANGED: no. The delta from 546b3e39 is exactly two files, docs/README.md (one line) and internal/workroom/fold_test.go (+42, one new test). No production code changed at all, so every enforcement conclusion I verified in round 1 carries to this head unchanged, and I re-ran the fold@4 loop-cut tests here to confirm: all nine pass.\n\nBASE AND CURRENT MAIN. Reporting plainly because it differs from the request text. This head is NOT rebased onto current main: its merge-base is 316d5aeb, so the branch is 546b3e39 plus the repair commit 076727fe. Main has also moved past the 06594a3b named in the conditions and is now 529758ca (cold-audit 06594a3b, checkpoint v2 d62e5e17, then two README commits). I judged this benign and verified it four ways rather than assuming. (1) git merge-tree --write-tree against current main 529758ca is clean, exit 0. (2) Main's changes since 316d5aeb are disjoint from the candidate: README.md at the repo root, internal/app/app.go, internal/gitstore, internal/kernel, internal/service/server_test.go and notes/. Main never touched internal/workroom, docs/, AGENTS.md or SKILL.md, so there is no semantic overlap with the loop cut, which is why the merge-tree is clean. (3) The round-1 architecture conclusion that depended on main still holds: internal/app/checkpoint.go on current main still stamps Profile from Workspace.foldProfile(), so the workroom-fold@4 bump still invalidates fold@3 checkpoints. (4) I built the trial merged tree (3b470a10) as a review-only unreferenced commit and ran it: go build and go vet pass, and internal/workroom, internal/app, internal/kernel and internal/gitstore all pass there. So the candidate is coherent against current main, not merely non-conflicting.\n\nARCHITECTURE: affected layer remains the Workroom application profile: governed kinds, deterministic fold, commitment lifecycle, fold-profile version. The contract changes and the change is declared in the same head, with docs/reference/architecture.md updating the lifecycle and merge-succession bullets and schema.go bumping workroom-fold@3 to @4. That bump is correctly wired to checkpoint invalidation, and this repository carries no gitseq/app-binding@0 record in refs/seq, so selectHost returns the default host and the bump forces a cold rebuild rather than the \"verifiable but uninterpretable\" refusal at internal/app/host.go:101. Kernel, gitstore and intent are untouched by the candidate. The cmd/gs merge gate is untouched: closure is purely projective and rides on the existing validateMergeReceiptNow. internal/statusview and the UI consume Commitment.Report only as an opaque chain member, so an artifact id in that field breaks no consumer. The contract change is declared, not silent.\n\nSECURITY: no authority boundary is weakened, and the repair strengthens the evidence. Artifact-as-report is tightly bound in latestCompletion: RenderArtifact, non-empty commit, record author equal to the promise author, and exactly one promise basis which must be that promise. Closure additionally requires a live sealed receipt whose plan came from validateMergeReceiptNow, which yields a plan only when the approval is effective, non-retired, ratified, verdict approved, head equal to the merge candidate, the artifact cited by that approval and standing at the candidate, artifact author different from approval author for independence, and receipt author equal to the artifact author. An implementer cannot self-close: artifacts carry SatisfierNone so they cannot be ratified at all, and the only other route to satisfied is an explicit report ratified by the originating requester. The board stays legible: promised, then reported with the requester waiting, then satisfied. The approval bases are written by the reviewer rather than the implementer and every admitted artifact must stand at the reviewed candidate, so a merge cannot close a commitment for unreviewed work. The cross-author boundary I flagged in round 1 is now both enforced and pinned by a regression test. Explicit pre-merge review ratification remains mandatory and is proven by mutation.\n\nSIMPLIFICATION: the cut stays clean and the repair adds no fluff. The README fix is one line and the regression is one focused test reusing the existing helpers. I swept the whole tree again for residual old-ceremony language: the only hits are the new explanatory text stating that no duplicate ready-for-review record is needed, the still-valid explicit-report path, and two dated historical notes (2026-08-06-bootstrap.md, 2026-08-09-connectors.md) which describe past acts and correctly need nothing.\n\nONE NON-BLOCKING RESIDUAL, recorded so it is not lost. docs/README.md still has a Recipes row reading \"Run a work loop | Request through review, merge, and ratification.\" Read as a sequence, that puts ratification after the merge, which is the step this cut removes. I deliberately did not block on it and I want the reasoning on the record, because I did block on the neighbouring row in round 1. The round-1 line asserted a superseded spine, \"Request, promise, report, ratification\", and omitted artifacts and merges entirely, so a reader took away a wholly wrong model. This row names only things that still exist, since the recipe genuinely covers ratifying the approval before merge, and its defect is an implied ordering rather than a false assertion; the page it points to states correctly that the approval was already ratified before merge and that no implementation report or ratification follows. Worth tidying in whatever next touches that file, not worth another round trip on this head.\n\nGATES, all run by me at this exact head in a clean worktree: go build ./... OK; go vet ./... OK; go test ./... -count=1 EXIT=0 with all 25 packages ok, including internal/docset 547.5s and internal/workroom 4.9s. The docset gate passed here despite a concurrent docset run from another worktree, so the contention-only timeout reported in the ready-for-review evidence did not reproduce for me. Trial merged tree against current main: go build OK, go vet OK, and internal/workroom, internal/app, internal/kernel and internal/gitstore all ok.\n\nVerified before promising: all four candidate artifacts effective, non-retired, non-stale, describes_superseded_world false, each standing at commit 076727fe; the round-1 candidate set is retired. Nothing was refused by the fold during this review.","body":{"artifact":"git:sha1:5d2622748872b7e2dec3fe5c59e4be73a35e0bc8#git:sha1:fa20f4c5119ad9c3f84a790e85a9dc0e570cec57","head":"076727fed7677931f173654a61a127975380e487","verdict":"approved"}}